VERDICT: PASS -- all three authored claims pass, and every one of them is graded
on the wire and in the dataplane rather than off `show vrrp`. The virtual router
is VRRPv3 by its own advertisements' version field, it answers for a virtual IP
that neither router owns using the RFC virtual MAC, the end device is reached
throughout the loss of either router, and the role transition is visible in the
forwarded traffic itself.
      (1) basic VRRP per the guide ...... configured and every default the guide
                                          states checked against the device:
                                          priority 100, preempt TRUE, the master
                                          answering ARP with the virtual MAC,
                                          accept mode on for v3, INIT on
                                          interface down
      v3, read off the wire ............. the advertisement's own version field
                                          says VRRPv3, with 00:00:5e:00:01:0a as
                                          the Ethernet SOURCE
      the virtual MAC is virtual ........ the ARP reply for 10.38.22.36 carries
                                          00:00:5e:00:01:0a, and the SAME capture
                                          carries both routers' real MACs for
                                          their real addresses
      (2) backup loses connection ....... swi_b vlan10 down -> Initialize; end
                                          device still reached 5/5 at ttl 63,
                                          control 0/5, instrument 5/5
      (2) master loses connection ....... monitored circuit down -> swi_b Master;
                                          end device still reached 5/5, at ttl 62
      (3) role transitioning ............ ttl 63 -> 62 -> 63 on the virtual-MAC
                                          flow across failover and preempt-back,
                                          with the instrument flow held at 63 and
                                          the unowned-MAC control at 0 throughout
      three mastership mechanisms ....... priority; session `disable`; and
                                          `circuit-failover vlan61 120` giving an
                                          exact 150 -> 30 decrement
      transition mode ................... `transition-mode true` emits a PAIRED
                                          v2 and v3 advertisement 26 us apart
      persistence ....................... survives a cold boot, tracking
                                          relationship re-armed, proven by frames
                                          arriving and not by a `show`
      removal ........................... back to the measured baseline exactly:
                                          no advertisements, no ARP reply, 0/5 on
                                          the virtual MAC, instrument still 5/5
      no errors, no crashes ............. neither device logged an error or
                                          restarted; both answered every console
                                          command throughout
CAVEAT:  What this run did NOT establish.
         - STEP 4 is measured evidence that two components of ONE firmware image
           disagree about the checksum of its own default VRRPv3-for-IPv4
           advertisement. WHICH of the two modes is the RFC 5798 conformant one
           is NOT settled by this bench and is deliberately not claimed: the
           validator is the DUT's own bundled `tcpdump`, and it was not
           independently verified. The interop question that follows -- whether
           a third-party RFC-conformant VRRPv3 router would discard the
           default-mode advertisements and become a second master -- is
           **NOT MEASURED**. The instrument it needs is a third-party conformant
           VRRPv3 peer, and this bench has none. What IS measured is bounded by
           two controls in STEP 4: default-to-default is fine, alternate-to-
           alternate is fine, and only the MISMATCH splits the pair.
         - ONE virtual router (VRID 10) on one VLAN, IPv4 only. `router ipv6
           vrrp` was not touched, and neither was a multi-VRID or multi-VLAN
           arrangement.
         - The advertisement interval was left at its default 1 s throughout;
           `advertisement-interval csec` exists and was not exercised. Preempt
           mode was exercised only at its default TRUE. Accept mode is not
           configurable for IPv4 on this platform, so it was read and not set.
         - `circuit-failover` was exercised with ONE monitored circuit and one
           delta (vlan61, 120). Master-down timing was not measured to sub-second
           resolution; the counted legs establish that the end device is reached
           across the transition, not how many packets a transition costs.

