  |  ip route 10.38.22.64/27 10.38.22.34        |
  +---------------------------------------------+

  THE ONE DESIGN CHOICE THAT CARRIES THE WHOLE CASE. Both host NICs land on the
  DUT, and swi_b has a static route to eth4's subnet VIA the DUT. So a frame
  addressed to the virtual MAC must HAIRPIN when swi_b is Master:

      DUT is Master    client -> swi_d -> eth4           ONE routing hop,  ttl 63
      swi_b is Master  client -> swi_b -> swi_d -> eth4  TWO routing hops, ttl 62

  Both routers answer with the SAME virtual MAC and the frames are otherwise
  byte-identical, so the received TTL is the only field on the wire that says
  WHICH router forwarded. That is what makes the role transition measurable in
  the dataplane instead of only in `show vrrp`.

  THE VIRTUAL IP IS OWNED BY NEITHER ROUTER. 10.38.22.36 was confirmed free
  before use (STEP 0: the identical ARP request drew no reply of any kind). So
  there is no address owner, no preferred master, the election is decided purely
  by priority, and the virtual MAC is in use in every leg.

  DUT     : swi_d (AT-x960-56YSQ) console u2 @115200, S/N A2WQS700B, standalone,
