  every probe:

awplus(config)# vrrp ?
  icmp-ttl-exceeded-error-use-physical-address
  vmac                                          Virtual-MAC feature
awplus(config)# router vrrp ?        -> <1-255>  Virtual router identifier
awplus(config)# router vrrp 10 ?     -> IFNAME   Interface name
awplus(config)# router vrrp 10 vlan10
awplus(config-router)# ?
  advertisement-interval   alternate-checksum-mode  circuit-failover
  disable  do  enable  exit  help  no  preempt-mode  priority  show
  transition-mode  virtual-ip
                                          <-- the complete 14-command list.
                                              There is NO `accept-mode` and NO
                                              `version` here
awplus(config-router)# virtual-ip 10.38.22.36 ?
  backup  Set default backup (Not IP address owner)
  master  Set default master (IP address owner)
awplus(config-router)# transition-mode ?
  false  Transition mode disabled
  true   Transition mode enabled
awplus(config-router)# advertisement-interval ?
  <1-40>  Interval (in seconds)
  csec    Use centiseconds rather than seconds
awplus(config-router)# version ?
% Unrecognized command

  HOW "v3" IS GRADED, AND WHY NOT FROM A CONFIG LINE. The case is titled
  VRRP(v3), and there is NO `version` command on this platform -- the probe
  above is the proof. The guide's own "VRRPv2 to VRRPv3 transition
  configuration" chapter upgrades a router by CHANGING ITS SOFTWARE RELEASE AND
  REBOOTING, not by a command. So "v3" cannot be read off the configuration at
  all, and a tester who looks for a config line will wrongly report the feature
  missing. It is graded three ways instead, all of them measured:
    - the VERSION FIELD of the advertisement, decoded from a capture (below);
    - the existence of `transition-mode {true|false}` and
      `alternate-checksum-mode`, which exist only because VRRPv3 does (probe
      above; `transition-mode` is exercised in STEP 6);
    - `Accept mode: TRUE` by default, which the guide (p.3) states is
      specifically the AlliedWare Plus VRRPv3 behaviour: "The VRRPv3 accept mode
      is enabled by default in the AlliedWare Plus VRRPv3 implementation". There
      is no `accept-mode` command in IPv4 `router vrrp` mode on this platform --
      it is on, reported, and not configurable here.

  THE CONFIGURATION, as sent. No command on either device was rejected:

    DUT (/dev/u2)                          swi_b (/dev/u3)
      configure terminal                     configure terminal
      router vrrp 10 vlan10                  router vrrp 10 vlan10
       virtual-ip 10.38.22.36 backup          virtual-ip 10.38.22.36 backup
       priority 150                           priority 100
       enable                                 enable
      end                                    end

