  Defaults checked against the guide: priority range 1-255, default 100 (p.7);
  preempt TRUE by default (p.7); advertisement interval 1 s.

  ** VRRPv3 READ OFF THE WIRE **

awplus# tcpdump -i vlan10 -c 3 -nn -v -e proto 112
05:59:32.403403 00:00:5e:00:01:0a > 01:00:5e:00:00:12, ethertype IPv4,
    length 46: (tos 0x0, ttl 255, id 53, proto VRRP (112), length 32)
    10.38.22.34 > 224.0.0.18: VRRPv3, Advertisement, vrid 10, prio 150,
    intvl 100cs, length 12, (bad vrrp cksum 1846), addrs: 10.38.22.36
  ... x3, exactly 1.000 s apart (05:59:32.403 / 33.403 / 34.405)

  Everything the case needs is in that one line:
    - version field    = **VRRPv3**, from the protocol's own header
    - vrid             = 10, advertising virtual IP 10.38.22.36
    - priority         = 150
    - interval         = 100 cs, i.e. the configured 1 s
    - Ethernet SOURCE  = **00:00:5e:00:01:0a**, the RFC virtual MAC for vrid 10,
                         and NOT either router's own address
    - IP source        = 10.38.22.34, which attributes the Master
    - destination      = 224.0.0.18 / 01:00:5e:00:00:12, TTL 255

  The `(bad vrrp cksum 1846)` was NOT written off as a decoder artefact. It is
  measured in STEP 4.

  => STEP 1 PASS: a basic VRRP per the guide is configured and running, and the
     "(v3)" in the case title is established from the advertisement's own
     version field rather than from any configuration line -- which is the only
     way it can be established on this platform, since no `version` command
     exists. The election went to the configured priority, the advertisements
     are sourced from the RFC virtual MAC at the configured interval, and both
     devices agree on who the master is.

STEP 2 -- the virtual MAC is the subject, and the same capture proves it is neither router's  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: COUNTED LEG V1-dut-master, which ARPs for all THREE addresses at once
  -- the virtual IP, the DUT's real address and swi_b's real address -- so the
  virtual MAC is discriminated against both real ones inside ONE capture.

  eth3:
    x5  ARP-REQ  who-has 10.38.22.36 tell 10.38.22.33
    x5  ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.36 is-at **00:00:5e:00:01:0a**
                                 [eth 00:00:5e:00:01:0a -> 00:11:22:33:44:01]
    x5  ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.34 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43   <-- the DUT's real SVI MAC
    x5  ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.35 is-at 00:00:cd:42:30:29   <-- swi_b's real MAC
    x14 IP p112 10.38.22.34 -> 224.0.0.18 ttl=255
                                 [eth 00:00:5e:00:01:0a -> ...]
  eth4:
    x5  UDP ...:9999 [ours] **ttl=63**   SUBJECT    via the virtual MAC, ONE hop
    x5  UDP ...:9997 [ours] ttl=63       INSTRUMENT
    x0  UDP ...:9998                     CONTROL

  => STEP 2 PASS: the ARP reply for the virtual IP carries 00:00:5e:00:01:0a in
     BOTH the ARP payload and the Ethernet source, and the same capture carries
     both routers' REAL MACs answering for their real addresses, so the virtual
     MAC is demonstrably neither of them. That matters specifically here because
     every SVI on this DUT shares one MAC: the virtual MAC is the only address
     on this segment that is not e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43. And the control addressed to
     an unowned MAC arrived 0 of 5 in the same leg, so the subject's 5 of 5 is
     routing to the virtual MAC and not flooding. Compare STEP 0, where the
     identical ARP request got no reply at all.

