  => STEP 3 PASS: the first half of authored claim (2). The backup router lost
     its connection to the VRRP segment and went to Initialize on its own
     admission, and the end device was reached 5 of 5 throughout at the
     unchanged one-hop TTL, with the unowned-MAC control at 0 and the instrument
     at 5 in the same capture. Nothing visible to the client changed at all --
     same virtual MAC in the ARP reply, same TTL at the far side -- which is the
     correct behaviour when the router that failed was not the forwarder.

STEP 4 -- "No unexpected errors ... are observed": the checksum of the default VRRPv3 advertisement, measured  done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Why this step exists: STEP 1's capture carried `(bad vrrp cksum 1846)`. That
  was not written off as a decoder artefact and not ignored. It was measured,
  and what the measurement shows is recorded here as evidence. THIS STEP IS NOT
  A CASE FAILURE: the split state below was DELIBERATELY INDUCED by a mismatched
  configuration, it never occurred in any default-mode leg, and both bounding
  controls are measured in this same step.

  THE A/B, IN ONE CAPTURE, ONE DECODER, ONE WINDOW. `alternate-checksum-mode`
  set on the DUT ONLY:

awplus# tcpdump -i vlan10 -c 2 -nn -v proto 112
06:02:47.574954 IP (tos 0x0, ttl 255, id 248, offset 0, flags [none], proto VRRP (112), length 32)
    10.38.22.34 > 224.0.0.18: VRRPv3, Advertisement, vrid 10, prio 150, intvl 100cs, length 12, addrs: 10.38.22.36
06:02:48.179982 IP (tos 0x0, ttl 255, id 14, offset 0, flags [none], proto VRRP (112), length 32)
    10.38.22.35 > 224.0.0.18: VRRPv3, Advertisement, vrid 10, prio 100, intvl 100cs, length 12, (bad vrrp cksum 4a46), addrs: 10.38.22.36
2 packets captured
                                          <-- 10.38.22.34 is the DUT, with
                                              `alternate-checksum-mode` ON: NO
                                              checksum complaint.
                                              10.38.22.35 is swi_b, in the
                                              DEFAULT mode: (bad vrrp cksum 4a46)

  Two frames, same instrument, same second, differing only in which checksum
  style the sender used. The one WITH `alternate-checksum-mode` validates clean;
  the one in the default mode does not.

  A THIRD, INDEPENDENT CONFIRMATION, from the transition-mode capture of STEP 6:
  with transition mode on, the SAME daemon emits a v2 and a v3 advertisement
  26 microseconds apart. The VRRPv2 frame validates clean; the VRRPv3 frame in
  the same pair does not. So the instrument is not simply broken for VRRP -- it
  is specifically the v3 checksum it rejects.

  THE SPLIT MASTER, MEASURED. With the modes mismatched, swi_b stopped accepting
  the DUT's advertisements and took over, although it is priority 100 against
  the DUT's 150:

awplus# show vrrp brief                                        (box time 19:03:03)
Interface  Grp Prio Own Pre State      Virtual IP
 vlan10     10  150   N   P Master     10.38.22.36
x960-28YSQ# show vrrp brief                                    (box time 19:03:29)
Interface  Grp Prio Own Pre State      Virtual IP
 vlan10     10  100   N   P Master     10.38.22.36
x960-28YSQ# show vrrp
VMAC enabled
Address family IPv4
VRRP Id: 10 on interface: vlan10
 State: AdminUp   - Master
 Virtual IP address: 10.38.22.36 (Not-owner)
 Priority is 100
 Advertisement interval: 100 centiseconds
 Preempt mode: TRUE
 Multicast membership on IPv4 interface vlan10: JOINED
 Transition mode: FALSE
 Accept mode: TRUE
 Master address: **10.38.22.35**          <-- swi_b believes it is the master

  A priority-100 router cannot displace a priority-150 router it can HEAR, so
  this is a receive-side rejection, and the checksum is the only thing that
  changed. The dataplane harm, counted (leg V3-split-master):

    x11 IP p112 **10.38.22.35** -> 224.0.0.18 [eth 00:00:5e:00:01:0a -> ...]
    x10 IP p112 **10.38.22.34** -> 224.0.0.18 [eth 00:00:5e:00:01:0a -> ...]
        ** both routers advertising for vrid 10 from the SAME virtual MAC **
    x10 ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.36 is-at 00:00:5e:00:01:0a
        ** TEN replies to FIVE requests -- both routers answered, and the client
           is steered by whichever reply lands last **
    eth4: x5 UDP ...:9999 ttl=63  -- the frames still got through, the DUT
                                     winning the L2 race for them; which router
                                     serves the client is a race, not a decision

  Two ports claiming one unicast MAC on one segment is the classic duplicate-MAC
  hazard, and `show vrrp` on each device looks perfectly healthy in isolation.

  THE TWO CONTROLS THAT BOUND IT, both measured, so the finding is not
  over-claimed:
    - DEFAULT-TO-DEFAULT IS FINE. Before any of this, with both devices in the
      default mode, swi_b read `State: AdminUp - Backup` AND
      `Master address: 10.38.22.34`. It can only know the master's address by
      receiving, validating and parsing the DUT's advertisements. So
      AW+-to-AW+ interop in the default is unaffected; this is not a defect that
      appears in a normal single-vendor deployment.
    - ALTERNATE-TO-ALTERNATE IS ALSO FINE. Setting `alternate-checksum-mode` on
      swi_b as well re-converged the pair immediately -- swi_b back to `Backup`
      with `Master address: 10.38.22.34`, and a capture then showed a single
      advertiser, 10.38.22.34, with no checksum complaint.
    - Reverting BOTH to the default re-converged the pair again.
    ** So it is the MISMATCH that splits the pair, not either mode. **

  WHAT IS *NOT* ESTABLISHED, AND MUST NOT BE READ INTO THIS. Which of the two
  modes is the RFC 5798 conformant one is NOT settled by this bench. The
  validator here is the DUT'S OWN BUNDLED `tcpdump`, and its correctness was not
  independently verified. The measurable, assumption-free statement is:

    ** two components of the same firmware image disagree about the checksum of
       its own default VRRPv3 advertisement, and the vendor ships a command that
       makes them agree. **

  The interop question -- whether a third-party RFC-conformant VRRPv3 router
  would discard the default-mode advertisements and become a second master -- is
  **NOT MEASURED**. The instrument it needs is a third-party conformant VRRPv3
  peer, and this bench has none. It is the open question, not a result. What IS
  measured is that a receiver which rejects these frames ends up as a second
  master, because the mismatched-mode experiment produced exactly that outcome
  between two AW+ routers.

  THE OPERATIONAL CAUTION THAT FOLLOWS, WHATEVER THE ANSWER:
  `alternate-checksum-mode` must be set on EVERY router in a virtual router or
  on NONE. Setting it on one -- a staged upgrade, or a mixed-vendor rollout --
  silently yields two masters for one virtual IP. The guide does not say so: the
  VRRP Feature Overview and Configuration Guide C613-22049-00 REV E, read in
  full, contains ZERO occurrences of "alternate" and ZERO of "checksum". The
  DUT's own help for the command is bare:

awplus(config-router)# alternate-checksum-mode ?
  <cr>

  => STEP 4 done: measured evidence, deliberately induced, with both bounding
     controls taken. The case's own claim (3) -- "no unexpected errors, crashes
     are observed" -- is unaffected: no device errored, logged a fault or
     restarted at any point here, and the split state was produced on purpose by
     a configuration the case then reverted, after which the pair re-converged.
     Both devices were returned to the default mode before STEP 5, and every
     later leg ran default-to-default.

STEP 5 -- "...when either the master ... loses connection" and "Role transitioning works correctly"  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the stimulus is VRRP's OWN failover trigger, `circuit-failover`, not a
  bare priority edit -- it is the mechanism the guide describes (p.25) for the
  master losing connection to the rest of the network, and unlike shutting the
  DUT's vlan10 or vlan48 it does not also destroy the measurement path.

  An ordering constraint found on the way, and worth recording because a
  stop-on-error config driver abandons the rest of its batch at that line:

awplus(config-router)# circuit-failover vlan61 120
% You must disable this session first

  so the working sequence is disable / configure / enable:

awplus(config)# router vrrp 10 vlan10
awplus(config-router)# disable
awplus(config-router)# circuit-failover vlan61 120
awplus(config-router)# enable
awplus# show vrrp
 Monitored circuit: vlan61, Priority Delta: 120, Status: UP
                                          <-- 150 - 120 = 30, below swi_b's 100,
                                              so failing vlan61 MUST hand over

  BEFORE:
awplus# show vrrp brief
 vlan10     10  150   N   P Master  10.38.22.36

  STIMULUS -- `shutdown` on the monitored circuit, echoed at box epoch
  1792130863.090:

awplus(config)# interface vlan61
awplus(config-if)# shutdown
awplus# show vrrp brief                    (read in the same instant)
 vlan10     10   **30**   N   P Master  10.38.22.36
                                          <-- the priority has ALREADY dropped by
                                              the configured delta; the role has
                                              not yet moved. An exact 150 -> 30

  AFTER:
x960-28YSQ# show vrrp brief
 vlan10     10  100   N   P **Master**  10.38.22.36
x960-28YSQ# show vrrp
 State: AdminUp   - Master      Priority is 100
 Master address: **10.38.22.35**
awplus# show vrrp brief
 vlan10     10   30   N   P **Backup**  10.38.22.36
                                          <-- exactly ONE master. A clean
                                              transition, not the split state of
                                              STEP 4

  ** THE ROLE TRANSITION, MEASURED IN THE DATAPLANE ** (leg V4-swib-master):

  eth3:
    x10 IP p112 **10.38.22.35** -> 224.0.0.18 ttl=255
                                 [eth 00:00:5e:00:01:0a -> 01:00:5e:00:00:12]
        <-- the advertisements now come from swi_b's address, still sourced from
            the SAME virtual MAC
    x5  ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.36 is-at **00:00:5e:00:01:0a**
        <-- unchanged. The client sees nothing at all, which is the point
  eth4:
    x5  UDP ...:9999 [ours] **ttl=62**   SUBJECT    -- TWO hops: swi_b -> swi_d
    x5  UDP ...:9997 [ours] **ttl=63**   INSTRUMENT -- ONE hop, unchanged
    x0  UDP ...:9998                     CONTROL

  RECOVERY -- the monitored circuit back up:

awplus(config)# interface vlan61
awplus(config-if)# no shutdown
awplus# show vrrp brief   ->  vlan10 10 150 N P Master 10.38.22.36
awplus# show vrrp         ->  Monitored circuit: vlan61, Priority Delta: 120,
                              Status: UP

  COUNTED LEG V5-preempt-back:
    x5 UDP ...:9999 [ours] **ttl=63**   <-- back to ONE hop
    x5 UDP ...:9997 [ours] ttl=63

  => STEP 5 PASS: the second half of authored claim (2) and the whole of claim
     (3), both read off the forwarded traffic. 62 against 63 IN THE SAME CAPTURE
     is the verdict: the subject flow to the virtual MAC gained exactly one
     routing hop while the instrument flow to the DUT's real MAC did not, so
     those frames are now being forwarded by swi_b and not by the DUT -- and the
     end device was reached 5 of 5 right through the master losing connection,
     with the unowned-MAC control at 0 throughout. The full round trip
     Master -> Backup -> Master was measured in the dataplane at every step, the
     priority decrement was the exact configured delta, and no error or crash
     occurred on either device. Three distinct mastership mechanisms have now
     been exercised: the priority election of STEP 1, the session `disable`
     above, and the monitored-circuit decrement here.

STEP 6 -- `transition-mode true`: paired VRRPv2 and VRRPv3 advertisements  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The guide p.26: "RFC 5798 for VRRPv3 allows a VRRPv3 virtual router to send
  both VRRPv2 and VRRPv3 advertisements ... Transition mode is disabled by
  default and is enabled by issuing the transition-mode true command."

  The same ordering constraint as STEP 5 applies:

awplus(config-router)# transition-mode true
% You must disable this session first

  so: `disable` / `transition-mode true` / `enable`, after which
  `show vrrp` reads `Transition mode: TRUE`.

awplus# tcpdump -i vlan10 -c 4 -nn -v -e proto 112
06:10:07.103828 00:00:5e:00:01:0a > 01:00:5e:00:00:12, length 46:
    (ttl 255, proto VRRP (112), length 32)
    10.38.22.34 > 224.0.0.18: **VRRPv3**, Advertisement, vrid 10, prio 150,
    intvl 100cs, length 12, (bad vrrp cksum 1846), addrs: 10.38.22.36
06:10:07.103854 00:00:5e:00:01:0a > 01:00:5e:00:00:12, length 54:
    (ttl 255, proto VRRP (112), length 40)
    10.38.22.34 > 224.0.0.18: **VRRPv2**, Advertisement, vrid 10, prio 150,
    authtype none, intvl 1s, length 20, addrs: 10.38.22.36
  ... and the same PAIR again at 06:10:08.104189 / .104214

  06:10:07.103854 - 06:10:07.103828 = **26 microseconds** between the two
  versions of the same advertisement period, from the same virtual MAC to the
  same group. The v2 frame carries 20 bytes of VRRP against v3's 12 (v2 carries
  the 8-byte auth field) and expresses the identical 1 s interval as `intvl 1s`
  instead of `intvl 100cs`. swi_b was unaffected and stayed Backup, as the guide
  says only the master needs transition mode.

  Reverted with `disable` / `transition-mode false` / `enable`.

  => STEP 6 PASS: transition mode does exactly what the guide describes -- every
     advertisement period emits BOTH versions, 26 us apart, from one virtual MAC
     to one group. This is also the cleanest single piece of evidence that the
     DEFAULT on this platform is v3: turning transition mode ON is what makes a
     v2 advertisement appear at all. With it off, the DUT sends v3 and only v3.

